  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/garra-rufa-2/",
      "html": "<ul><li>Maximum size: around 10 cm TL</li><li>Temperature: 16–30°C</li><li>Very easy to care for</li><li>Should definitely be kept in a shoal</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/garra-rufa-2/",
      "html": "<p>The Kangal psoriasis fish (Garra rufa) is widespread in the Middle East. The population living in the Turkish Kangal thermal baths has become famous.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/garra-rufa-2/",
      "html": "<p>Growth, i.e. algae and the microorganisms it contains, form the natural diet of the Kangal psoriasis fish. This sucking barb grazes on algae-covered stones.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/garra-rufa-2/",
      "html": "<p>This sucking barb is not shy towards humans and grazes on them, as it usually does with algae-covered stones. In humans, the grazing process very gently removes dead skin cells and thus promotes the healing of inflamed areas. Young fish in particular are very fond of cleaning and eating algae. Garra rufa should definitely be kept in a shoal.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/garra-rufa-2/",
      "html": "<p>The population of this species living in the Turkish Kangal thermal baths can be used to provide relief for people suffering from serious skin diseases (neurodermatitis, psoriasis, etc.). In a self-experiment, the small Garra rufa nibbled on an arm as if it were a root. Garra rufa can be recommended without hesitation as a great algae eater for the aquarium, and it swims voluntarily to the hand as soon as it is put in the aquarium.</p>"
    }
  ],
